course: dictionary
lesson: 4352
title: Names with a capital letter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are the name of one special person, place or thing. These are called proper nouns, and they always start with a capital letter. Guinness is one of these words, so it is a name, not an ordinary word like "record".
explain: The same name can mean different things, so use the context to decide. In «Some amazing world records are real and are checked by Guinness World Records; other amazing photos are edited.», Guinness World Records is the name of a book and organisation that checks records. The clue words world records and checked tell you which one is meant.
explain: A family name can also be a proper noun. Arthur Guinness is a person's name, and it starts with a capital letter too. Look at the words around the name and you will know which Guinness the writer means.
fact: A proper noun is the name of a particular person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter.
fact: Guinness World Records is a book and organisation that checks and lists world records, such as the tallest person or the fastest runner.
fact: Guinness is also an Irish family name, for example Arthur Guinness, who started the brewery in Dublin.
fact: Guinness is also the name of a dark Irish drink, made in Dublin since 1759; it is for adults, and children should never drink alcohol.
exercise: «Some amazing world records are real and are checked by Guinness World Records; other amazing photos are edited.» What is Guinness World Records? | A type of camera | A dark beer made in Dublin | *A book and organisation that checks world records | A tall building in a city
exercise: Why does Guinness start with a capital letter? | Because it is a type of food | Because it is the first word of every sentence | Because it is a very long word | *Because it is the name of a particular thing or person
exercise: Ben says, «I want to break a Guinness world record for the fastest runner.» What does break a Guinness world record mean? | To stop running in a race | To drop a glass of drink | To lose a race on purpose | *To beat the best result ever recorded and listed in the book
test: In «Arthur Guinness started the brewery», what kind of word is Guinness? | A verb, an action word | A number | *A proper noun, the name of a person | A describing word, an adjective
test: Which of these would Guinness World Records be most likely to list? | A recipe for toast | *The tallest person in the world | The name of your teacher | The best colour for a bedroom
exam: Jayden reads «Guinness» in a story about a beer and a book of records and asks, "Which one is it?" What should he do? | Ask for a dictionary with no names in it | Always pick the beer, because it is the first one he thought of | *Look at the words around it, such as beer or world records, to see which Guinness is meant | Skip the word and never read it again
